In a multi-threaded PascalABC.NET program, an exception can be handled by placing the code that may throw an exception within a try-catch block. This ensures that any exception thrown within the try block is caught and handled gracefully, preventing the program from crashing.

To ensure thread safety and prevent deadlocks, you can use locking mechanisms such as the lock statement or Monitor class to synchronize access to shared resources.

Here's an example of a try-catch block that demonstrates exception handling while ensuring thread safety and preventing deadlocks:

```csharp
// Shared resource
private static int sharedValue = 0;
private static readonly object lockObject = new object();

// Thread method
private static void ThreadMethod()
{
    try
    {
        // Lock the shared resource
        lock (lockObject)
        {
            // Access the shared resource
            sharedValue++;

            // Simulate some work that may throw an exception
            if (sharedValue % 2 == 0)
            {
                throw new Exception("Even value detected");
            }
        }
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        // Handle the exception
        Console.WriteLine($"Exception caught: {ex.Message}");
    }
}

// Main method
public static void Main(string[] args)
{
    // Create and start multiple threads
    var threads = new List<Thread>();
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        var thread = new Thread(ThreadMethod);
        threads.Add(thread);
        thread.Start();
    }

    // Wait for all threads to complete
    foreach (var thread in threads)
    {
        thread.Join();
    }

    // Output the final value of the shared resource
    Console.WriteLine($"Shared value: {sharedValue}");
}
```

In this example, the sharedValue variable is a resource accessed by multiple threads. By using the lock statement with the lockObject as the lock parameter, access to the sharedValue is synchronized, ensuring that only one thread can modify it at a time.

Within the try block, the sharedValue is incremented and then checked for evenness. If an even value is detected, an exception is thrown. The catch block then handles the exception by printing the exception message.

By using the lock statement, the code ensures thread safety while preventing deadlocks, as only one thread can access the shared resource at a time. The try-catch block allows for graceful exception handling, preventing the program from crashing.
